The expression $f(a,b,c) = (ab-c^2)(bc-a^2)(ca-b^2)$ can be rewritten using the constraint $a^2+b^2+c^2=3$. By substituting $c^2 = 3 - a^2 - b^2$ (and cyclically permuting the variables for the other factors), we transform the quadratic factors in the original expression into forms involving only $a, b, c$ and constants. Specifically, the factors are rewritten as:
\begin{align*} ab - c^2 &= ab - (3 - a^2 - b^2) = a^2 + ab + b^2 - 3 \\ bc - a^2 &= bc - (3 - b^2 - c^2) = b^2 + bc + c^2 - 3 \\ ca - b^2 &= ca - (3 - a^2 - c^2) = a^2 + ca + c^2 - 3 \end{align*}
Thus, the expression simplifies to $f(a,b,c) = (a^2 + ab + b^2 - 3)(b^2 + bc + c^2 - 3)(a^2 + ac + c^2 - 3)$. While this form does not immediately resolve into a product of simple differences, it is the most direct algebraic rewriting achieved using the constraint.

